architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "sheni_09"
  title: Every primitive gets a soft twin except `string`, which cannot spell its own unknown
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-29"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    ADR sheni_08 settled what `soft_` means and left the family at two members
    with a third deferred. It also said the rule tells you when not to write
    one, and expected the family to stay small.

    Applying the rule to the primitive group says otherwise, and the reason is
    in ADR sheni_03. That record gave the numerics a fallback of zero, and
    called it an identity rather than a sentinel. ADR sheni_08 then drew the
    line that makes an identity acceptable: the empty string is also what "set
    to nothing" means, so nothing is lost when absence and emptiness coincide.

    Zero does not pass that test. Zero is an answer. A count of zero items, a
    balance of zero, a temperature of zero -- each is a fact somebody
    established, and none of them means nobody looked. An optional `u8` field
    falling back to zero is the same conflation ADR sheni_03 refused for
    `bool`, admitted for the numerics because zero looked empty enough.

    So the gap ADR sheni_06 and ADR sheni_07 each closed for one type is open
    across most of group 1, and the closure is the same closure every time.
    That is a family, not three special cases.
  decision: |
    A soft twin for every primitive whose unknown can be written without
    taking a value away from the type. That is fifteen of the sixteen:
    `soft_bool`, `soft_u8` through `soft_u128`, `soft_i8` through `soft_i128`,
    `soft_f32`, `soft_f64`, `soft_byte`, and `soft_char`.

    The unknown is spelled `unknown`, case-insensitively, as `bool` already
    accepts its own spellings case-insensitively. It reads back canonically
    lowercase. A soft primitive therefore accepts exactly what its strict twin
    accepts, plus that one word, and nothing else changes: the same shape
    rules, the same range checks, the same codes. ADR sheni_08 forbids a
    `soft_` type from being more lenient, and this family is the test of that
    rule at scale -- fifteen types, none of which relaxes a single check.

    `soft_string` is not offered, and the exclusion is discovered rather than
    chosen. `string` accepts any well-formed UTF-8 verbatim, so no text lies
    outside its value space, so no word can mean "not known" without also
    being a string somebody meant literally. That is the definition of a
    sentinel, and ADR sheni_08 forbids exactly it. The second argument arrives
    at the same place from the other direction: an empty string is a genuine
    identity by ADR sheni_08's own test, so `string` is the one primitive that
    did not need a soft twin anyway. A rule that excludes nothing is
    decoration, and this is the rule doing work.

    The soft twins stay in group 1. Softness is a property of the value space
    and not a kind, which ADR sheni_08 settled, so a soft primitive is a
    primitive exactly as `soft_date` is a simple type.

    That places `soft_bool` in group 1, where ADR sheni_07 put it in group 3.
    ADR sheni_07 is amended rather than superseded, since it is still proposed
    and everything it decided about the type is unchanged: the same three
    values, the same order, the same operations. What changes is that it is no
    longer a bespoke enum. It is the boolean member of a uniform family, and
    Kleene's `and`, `or` and `not` ship on that member alone.

    The ordering argument survives the move. `false` ranks 0, `unknown` 1, and
    `true` 2, so `and` is the minimum, `or` the maximum, and `not` the
    complement against 2. In ADR sheni_07 that ranking was the enum's storage
    backing; here it is a function over the value. The arithmetic is what
    mattered, not where the number was kept.

    An unknown value carries the type it is unknown at, so an unknown `u8` and
    an unknown `bool` are different values. This crate already holds that the
    type is part of the value rather than something recoverable from it, which
    is why a `u8` and a `u64` holding seven are not equal, and an unknown that
    forgot its type would be the one value in the crate that broke the rule.

    Writing `unknown` at a strict type takes its own code, 115, rather than
    being reported as a malformed boolean or a two-character `char`. The
    document is not gibberish; it is reaching for a type that exists under
    another name, and saying so is the difference between a fixable message
    and a baffling one. The exception is `string`, where `unknown` is text and
    always was.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - An optional numeric field stops falling back to zero, which was a sentinel that ADR sheni_03 admitted by calling it an identity.
      - The family is generated from one rule rather than argued type by type, so the fifteenth member costs no decisions the first did not already settle.
      - A soft primitive accepts its strict twin's forms unchanged, which makes ADR sheni_08's rule that softness is not leniency checkable rather than aspirational.
      - The `string` exclusion demonstrates the rule has teeth, and it falls out of the type's own definition rather than from a judgement call.
      - `soft_bool` stops being a bespoke enum and becomes one member of a family, so there is one mechanism for softness in group 1 instead of two.
      - An unknown carrying its type keeps the crate's existing rule that the type is part of the value.
      - Reaching for `unknown` at a strict type produces a message that names the type the author wanted.
    negative:
      - Group 1 grows from sixteen names to thirty-one, and a schema author now chooses between twins for almost every scalar field.
      - Some will take the soft twin as a default because it is more forgiving, which is the same pressure ADR sheni_06 and ADR sheni_07 each recorded, now multiplied across the group.
      - The word `unknown` becomes reserved at fifteen types, so a `char` field can no longer explain a rejection by saying only that a char is one character.
      - Amending ADR sheni_07's group placement means a record's decision moved while it was still proposed, and a reader of the two records has to notice the amendment.
      - `soft_string` will be asked for repeatedly, and the answer is a two-step argument rather than a rule anyone will remember.
      - Kleene's operations now live on one member of a general family rather than on a type of their own, so the family's value enum has methods that are meaningful for one of its fifteen instantiations.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Offer `soft_string`, with `unknown` as its unknown
      reason: It takes the text "unknown" away from a type defined as accepting any text verbatim. That is a sentinel, which ADR sheni_08 forbids, and `string` already has an honest identity fallback in the empty string.
    -
      name: Offer `soft_string`, with the unknown reachable only by omitting the key
      reason: It leaves a member of the value space unwritable, so a document could not say what a schema could declare. A type whose values cannot all be written is worse than a type that is not offered.
    -
      name: Keep the family to the types with a demonstrated need, as ADR sheni_08 expected
      reason: The need is demonstrated for every numeric by the same argument that demonstrated it for `bool`. Picking a subset would mean deciding that some counts may be unknown and others may not, which is a schema author's question and not this crate's.
    -
      name: A generic `Soft<T>` over any type, rather than a family over group 1
      reason: ADR sheni_08 rejected the wrapper as the general model, and that stands -- `soft_date` is not a `date` plus an unknown. Within group 1 the wrapper shape happens to fit, and the implementation is uniform, but the name stays a prefix on a type so the two families read alike.
    -
      name: Let empty text mean unknown, so no word has to be reserved
      reason: Empty text already means an empty `string` and fails everywhere else, and ADR sheni_01 keeps that. Absence of the key is what means unknown, which ADR sheni_03 established, and empty text is a value rather than an absence.
    -
      name: Report `unknown` at a strict type as a malformed literal
      reason: It is the one wrong literal whose author can be told exactly which type they wanted. Spending a code on that is the same call ADR sheni_06 made at 235.
